---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import Tracer from "../components/Tracer";
---

<ToolPage tool="trace">
    <Fragment slot="lead">
        Drop in a photo, a scan or a logo and get <strong class="text-slate-300">real vector paths</strong> back —
        the boundary of every dark region, or one line down the middle of every stroke. Every slider is live, so the
        preview follows you as you drag it rather than after you commit.
    </Fragment>

    <Tracer client:load />

    <section slot="notes" class="relative px-4 pb-24">
        <div class="mx-auto w-[min(72rem,100%)]">
            <div class="grid gap-5 lg:grid-cols-3">
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Outline or centreline</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">Outline</strong> traces the boundary of everything darker than
                        the threshold, holes included, so a filled logo comes out as shapes with their counters intact. <strong class="text-slate-300">Centreline</strong> peels each stroke down to a one-pixel
                        skeleton first, so a 2 mm pen line becomes a single path to engrave rather than a long thin
                        outline to cut around.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 110ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Judge it, don't guess it</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        The source image sits faded behind the vectors, so moving the threshold shows you immediately
                        what is being kept and what is being dropped. Turn on <em>show points</em> to see what Optimize
                        is really costing you, and read the accuracy figure underneath — it is the largest deviation
                        actually accepted, measured on the path, not a restatement of the slider.
                    </p>
                </article>
                <article class="glass reveal rounded-2xl p-6" style="--reveal-delay: 220ms">
                    <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Corners stay corners</h2>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                        Smoothing is decided per corner, from how sharply the path bends relative to the span it cuts
                        across — a scale-free measure. So at the default a traced circle becomes curves while a traced
                        square comes out as four nodes and four right angles, instead of everything being rounded
                        equally.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <div class="glass reveal mt-5 rounded-2xl p-6">
                <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white">Getting a clean trace</h2>
                <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-400">
                    <li>
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">Start with the threshold.</strong> Everything else is polish. If
                        the shape is missing or the whole image fills in, that slider is the reason — and <em>invert</em> is the fix for light artwork on a dark ground.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">A JPEG traces worse than a PNG.</strong> Its compression
                        speckles the edges, and every speck becomes its own path. Raise <em>ignore smaller than</em> until they are gone.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">Big images are traced at a working size</strong> — 1600 px for
                        outlines, 900 for centrelines — so the sliders stay live. The physical size is unaffected: it
                        comes from the source pixels, and you can override it outright.
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <strong class="text-slate-300">Photographs are not line art.</strong> A single threshold
                        through a continuous-tone photo gives you a high-contrast silhouette, which is sometimes
                        exactly what you want and sometimes nothing like it. For tonal engraving, feed the photo to
                        your laser software as an image and let it dither instead.
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </div>

        </div>
    </section>
</ToolPage>
